  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "Rear-end collisions are a common cause of road trauma, yet the speed thresholds at which injury becomes probable remain poorly defined for safety interventions. This study quantifies the relationship between striking-vehicle speed, relative impact (closing) speed, and the severity of occupant injury in rear-end crashes. Using the US Crash Investigation Sampling System (CISS, 2016–2024) with Event Data Recorder (EDR) measurements, we developed binary logistic regression models for the probability of minor (MAIS 1), moderate (MAIS 2) and serious-or-fatal (MAIS 3+F) injury as functions of striking speed ( V following ) and relative impact speed ( V impact ). Both unweighted and CISS-weighted (population-representative) models were fitted on a sample of 216 two-vehicle rear-end crashes. Minor injuries occurred frequently even at near-zero speeds (30%–40% probability) and showed minimal speed dependence. Moderate-injury risk rose sharply above 40–60 km/h, reaching 10% probability at approximately 43 km/h ( V impact ) and 64 km/h ( V following ) in the weighted models. Serious-injury risk remained below 1% until approximately 7 km/h ( V impact ) and 22 km/h ( V following ), reaching 10% at approximately 88 km/h and 112 km/h respectively. Weighting for the CISS sampling design raised the estimated thresholds substantially, underscoring the importance of population-representative inference. These findings provide evidence-based targets for speed management, autonomous emergency braking calibration, and vehicle safety design consistent with Safe System and Vision Zero objectives.",
