  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis is based on a relatively small sample size of 216 crashes, which may limit the precision of the risk curves at extreme speeds.",
      "what": "This study analyzed 216 two-vehicle rear-end crashes from the US Crash Investigation Sampling System (2016–2024) using Event Data Recorder measurements to model injury risk. Binary logistic regression models calculated the probability of minor, moderate, and serious-or-fatal injuries based on both striking-vehicle speed and relative impact speed. The findings show that moderate-injury risk reaches a 10% probability at an impact speed of 43 km/h, while serious-injury risk hits the 10% threshold at an impact speed of 88 km/h.",
      "why": "It establishes empirical, population-weighted speed thresholds for rear-end crash injuries, correcting previous unweighted estimates that overrepresented crash severity. These specific velocity limits provide concrete benchmarks for calibrating autonomous emergency braking systems and setting urban speed limits under Safe System frameworks."
    }
  },
